WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.360
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:04.560
We have used a coil's magnetic moment to predict torque.

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:08.720
Now we ask what field a magnetic dipole creates around itself.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:13.060
A bar magnet and a small current loop can produce the same leading field

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:16.240
pattern at large distances if their magnetic moments match.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:20.760
For a bar magnet, the moment points from its south end toward its north end.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:25.000
For a current loop, the right hand rule gives the normal, and the moment

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.160
magnitude is current times enclosed area.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.500
The moment is not the same thing as the local magnetic field at every point.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:36.480
Outside a bar magnet, field lines leave the north region and curve toward the

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:36.940
south region.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.400
Inside the magnet, they continue from south toward north.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.400
They do not simply stop at the poles.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:46.900
In the standard magnetism model, the net magnetic flux through any closed

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:47.840
surface is zero.

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:52.560
Unlike the electric field of an isolated charge, a bar magnet's field has no

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.020
separate magnetic source or sink.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.400
The tangent to a field line gives the local field direction.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:01.560
It does not directly give the force direction on a moving charge, which still

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:02.980
requires velocity cross B.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:07.240
Our quantitative dipole formulas describe points far outside a localized

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:09.940
source, at distances much larger than its size.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:14.160
Close to a real magnet, shape and magnetization details matter.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:19.120
Treat the dipole as an approximation with a useful range, not a tiny drawing

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:20.820
that makes every nearby field exact.

00:01:21.264 --> 00:01:25.364
Put a bar magnet horizontally, south on the left and north on the right.

00:01:25.904 --> 00:01:27.064
Its moment points right.

00:01:27.564 --> 00:01:30.504
The axial line passes through both poles and the center.

00:01:31.224 --> 00:01:35.190
At a point far to the right of the north end, the field points right, away

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:35.404
from

00:01:35.404 --> 00:01:36.044
the north region.

00:01:36.844 --> 00:01:39.064
What about a point far to the left of the south end?

00:01:39.684 --> 00:01:41.004
Take a moment to draw the arrow.

00:01:41.484 --> 00:01:44.444
The field points toward the south region, which is also right.

00:01:44.624 --> 00:01:48.704
So the far axial field is parallel to the moment on both sides.

00:01:49.264 --> 00:01:51.364
It is not automatically outward from the center.

00:01:52.124 --> 00:01:57.164
Its magnitude is mu zero over four pi, multiplied by two times moment divided

00:01:57.164 --> 00:01:57.984
by r cubed.

00:01:58.724 --> 00:02:02.624
Here r is distance from the center of the localized dipole, not distance from

00:02:02.624 --> 00:02:03.304
the nearest pole.

00:02:04.044 --> 00:02:09.144
In vacuum or approximately air, use mu zero over four pi approximately ten to

00:02:09.144 --> 00:02:10.884
the minus seven in SI units.

00:02:11.824 --> 00:02:16.064
Suppose moment is zero point eight ampere meter squared and the axial point is

00:02:16.064 --> 00:02:20.244
zero point four meters from the center, sufficiently far compared with the

00:02:20.244 --> 00:02:20.864
magnet's size.

00:02:21.924 --> 00:02:25.604
Cube zero point four to get zero point zero six four.

00:02:25.604 --> 00:02:27.644
The field is two point five microtesla.

00:02:28.664 --> 00:02:30.304
Double the distance while staying on the axis.

00:02:30.784 --> 00:02:34.364
The field becomes one eighth as large, zero point three one two five

00:02:34.364 --> 00:02:35.104
microtesla.

00:02:35.744 --> 00:02:38.884
That cube dependence is characteristic of the far dipole field.

00:02:39.624 --> 00:02:42.875
Always check geometry, distance reference and the approximation before

00:02:42.875 --> 00:02:43.176
substituting numbers.

00:02:43.176 --> 00:02:48.336
Now move to the perpendicular bisector of the magnet, called its equatorial

00:02:48.336 --> 00:02:49.236
line in our drawing.

00:02:50.176 --> 00:02:51.556
Keep the moment pointing right.

00:02:52.356 --> 00:02:56.510
At a distant point above the center, symmetry cancels the vertical parts of

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:56.676
the

00:02:56.676 --> 00:02:59.036
field, while the horizontal parts point left.

00:02:59.696 --> 00:03:01.476
The same happens below the center.

00:03:02.536 --> 00:03:05.196
Thus the equatorial field is opposite to the moment.

00:03:05.736 --> 00:03:10.776
Its magnitude is mu zero over four pi, times moment divided by r cubed.

00:03:11.876 --> 00:03:15.376
Compared at the same center distance in the far field limit, the axial

00:03:15.376 --> 00:03:17.756
magnitude is twice the equatorial magnitude.

00:03:18.696 --> 00:03:22.355
For our zero point eight ampere meter squared moment at zero point four

00:03:22.355 --> 00:03:22.716
meters,

00:03:23.476 --> 00:03:27.416
equatorial field is one point two five microtesla, pointing left.

00:03:28.016 --> 00:03:32.196
The axial value at that same distance is two point five microtesla, pointing

00:03:32.196 --> 00:03:32.576
right.

00:03:33.076 --> 00:03:36.996
The equatorial field is not zero just because the point lies on a symmetry

00:03:36.996 --> 00:03:37.376
line.

00:03:38.316 --> 00:03:40.816
Symmetry can cancel one component while leaving another.

00:03:41.836 --> 00:03:45.732
Also, the ratio of two is not a rule for arbitrary points near a finite

00:03:45.732 --> 00:03:46.116
magnet.

00:03:47.216 --> 00:03:47.656
Distance matters.

00:03:48.396 --> 00:03:52.576
If an axial point is twice as far from the center as an equatorial point, its

00:03:52.576 --> 00:03:56.456
field magnitude is two divided by eight, or one quarter of the equatorial

00:03:56.456 --> 00:03:56.756
value.

00:03:57.236 --> 00:04:01.638
If the question says equal fields instead, the axial distance must be cube

00:04:01.638 --> 00:04:01.876
root

00:04:01.876 --> 00:04:04.076
of two times the equatorial distance.

00:04:04.916 --> 00:04:07.536
Do the cube scaling before reaching for a familiar ratio.

00:04:07.776 --> 00:04:12.756
Let's test the approximation using a source we can calculate: a thin circular

00:04:12.756 --> 00:04:13.976
current loop in vacuum.

00:04:14.616 --> 00:04:20.116
On its axis at distance x from the center, the field magnitude is mu zero I

00:04:20.456 --> 00:04:25.716
R squared divided by two times the quantity R squared plus x squared raised to

00:04:25.876 --> 00:04:26.336
three halves.

00:04:26.876 --> 00:04:30.896
This is the circular loop result, not a universal exact bar magnet formula.

00:04:31.616 --> 00:04:36.036
When x is much larger than loop radius R, the denominator is approximately two

00:04:36.316 --> 00:04:37.396
x cubed.

00:04:37.396 --> 00:04:42.456
Replace I pi R squared by the loop's moment, and the expression becomes the

00:04:42.456 --> 00:04:44.196
same far axial dipole field.

00:04:44.916 --> 00:04:47.436
We can even check how close the approximation is.

00:04:48.236 --> 00:04:52.456
Exact loop field divided by its far field estimate is the quantity one plus R

00:04:52.456 --> 00:04:55.256
squared over x squared raised to minus three halves.

00:04:55.896 --> 00:05:01.056
At x equal to ten radii, this ratio is about zero point nine eight five.

00:05:01.056 --> 00:05:04.276
The exact value is about ninety eight point five percent of the estimate.

00:05:04.776 --> 00:05:09.796
At x equal to one radius, the ratio is only about zero point three five four.

00:05:09.796 --> 00:05:11.036
That is not a small correction.

00:05:11.736 --> 00:05:17.086
At the center, x is zero and the actual loop field is mu zero I divided by two

00:05:17.086 --> 00:05:18.116
R, which is finite.

00:05:18.676 --> 00:05:22.276
The point dipole formula would diverge there because we have used it outside

00:05:22.276 --> 00:05:22.796
its range.

00:05:23.296 --> 00:05:27.436
A formula's apparent infinity can be a warning about its model, not a claim

00:05:27.436 --> 00:05:29.476
that a real magnet produces infinite field.

00:05:29.876 --> 00:05:34.297
For a finite bar magnet, choose a suitable source model or supplied result

00:05:34.297 --> 00:05:34.536
when

00:05:34.536 --> 00:05:35.996
the far field condition is absent.

00:05:36.384 --> 00:05:37.804
Finish with two sources.

00:05:38.684 --> 00:05:42.644
Our dipole has moment zero point eight ampere meter squared pointing right.

00:05:43.604 --> 00:05:47.844
Add a uniform external field of one point two five microtesla pointing right.

00:05:48.244 --> 00:05:52.437
At an equatorial point zero point four meters from the center, the dipole

00:05:52.437 --> 00:05:52.724
field

00:05:52.724 --> 00:05:54.924
is one point two five microtesla left.

00:05:55.364 --> 00:05:58.224
The two fields cancel there in our far field model.

00:05:59.004 --> 00:06:03.164
At an axial point at the same center distance, the dipole field is two point

00:06:03.164 --> 00:06:04.604
five microtesla right.

00:06:05.484 --> 00:06:08.944
Add the external field to get three point seven five microtesla right.

00:06:09.684 --> 00:06:13.764
One source has not switched off; its vector contribution cancels another only

00:06:13.764 --> 00:06:14.984
at the specified location.

00:06:15.944 --> 00:06:19.884
Now change the external field to point upward, keeping its magnitude one point

00:06:19.884 --> 00:06:21.004
two five microtesla.

00:06:21.764 --> 00:06:26.004
At the equatorial point, we have equal perpendicular components: left from the

00:06:26.004 --> 00:06:27.984
dipole and up from the external field.

00:06:28.424 --> 00:06:33.004
The resultant magnitude is one point two five times square root of two, about

00:06:33.004 --> 00:06:34.944
one point seven seven microtesla.

00:06:35.804 --> 00:06:39.664
It points forty five degrees above the leftward direction, not upward along

00:06:39.664 --> 00:06:40.524
either source alone.

00:06:40.944 --> 00:06:44.884
A small compass aligns with the local resultant field in a nonzero field

00:06:44.884 --> 00:06:45.184
region.

00:06:45.664 --> 00:06:49.184
At an exact zero, that field alone supplies no preferred alignment.

00:06:50.004 --> 00:06:51.664
Other fields and disturbances can matter.

00:06:52.564 --> 00:06:56.324
Keep the three decisions separate: identify axial or equatorial geometry,

00:06:57.144 --> 00:07:00.484
verify distance is large relative to source size, and add vectors.

00:07:01.444 --> 00:07:04.284
We have connected coils, bar magnets and dipole fields.

00:07:05.284 --> 00:07:08.664
Next we will examine how different materials respond to an applied magnetic

00:07:08.664 --> 00:07:08.984
field.
